Scenario ID: S01
Scenario title: Verification Test Setup and Reporting
Initial workflow description:
An engineer prepares a verification test, runs the evaluation, checks the results, and writes a short report. If something looks wrong, the engineer investigates and shares the result with related people.

Domain expert answers:
{
  "answers": [
    {
      "question_id": "Q1",
      "question": "Which specific tools or systems does the engineer use to run the evaluation and store the test results?",
      "answer": "The engineer uses a test execution tool to run the evaluation and a result repository to store the test result files. A report template is also used for documentation. Specific product names for these systems are not specified."
    },
    {
      "question_id": "Q2",
      "question": "What criteria or thresholds determine that a result 'looks wrong' and triggers the investigation branch?",
      "answer": "The workflow checks two conditions: first, whether result files are complete and readable (missing or corrupted files trigger an exception), and second, whether results contain abnormal patterns. Abnormal patterns trigger further investigation. Specific numeric thresholds or pattern definitions are not specified."
    },
    {
      "question_id": "Q3",
      "question": "What does the investigation step involve, and how is it concluded — is there a defined resolution condition or sign-off required?",
      "answer": "When abnormal patterns are found, the verification engineer investigates and summarizes findings in the report. The review engineer then reviews the report and may request clarification if needed. A formal sign-off condition or explicit resolution criteria are not specified beyond the review step."
    },
    {
      "question_id": "Q4",
      "question": "What is the required format and delivery destination of the short report, and who are the 'related people' who receive the findings?",
      "answer": "The report is produced using a report template and shared via an email or collaboration tool. Recipients include a review engineer and a project owner. Specific format requirements beyond using the template are not specified."
    },
    {
      "question_id": "Q5",
      "question": "How frequently is this verification test workflow executed, and is it triggered on a fixed schedule, by an event, or on demand?",
      "answer": "The workflow is initiated by a test request, suggesting it is event- or demand-driven. Fixed schedule or frequency details are not specified."
    },
    {
      "question_id": "Q6",
      "question": "Which step in the workflow currently takes the most time or manual effort to complete?",
      "answer": "The most effort-intensive manual steps are result file collection, result validity checking, and report drafting — all three are identified as automation opportunities due to their repetitive or rule-based nature. A single step ranked as most time-consuming is not explicitly specified."
    }
  ]
